15. Central Import and Export Engine.
16. Central Reporting and Analytics Engine.
17. Central Financial Settings.
18. Central Company and Branch Registry.

Do not create duplicate engines for different modules.

## 7.2 Mandatory Accounting Relationships

- Sales and invoices → Accounts Receivable → Receipt → Voucher → General Ledger.
- Purchases and supplier bills → Accounts Payable → Payment → Voucher → General Ledger.
- Inventory movement → Inventory Valuation → COGS → General Ledger.
- Fixed assets → Depreciation → Expense and Asset Ledger.
- Cashbook → Cash Ledger → General Ledger.
- Bank transactions → Bank Reconciliation → Bank Ledger.
- Loan transactions → EMI Schedule → Interest and Liability Ledger.
- GST/TDS → Tax Ledger → Compliance Reports.
- Franchise revenue → Royalty Calculation → Settlement → Ledger.
- Budget → Actual Transactions → Variance Reports.
- Financial Closing → Verified Ledger Balances → Financial Statements.
- Customer payment → Payment Verification → Receipt → Voucher → Ledger.
- Supplier payment → Payment Verification → Payment Voucher → Ledger.
- Salary processing → Payroll Approval → Salary Payment → Ledger.
- Investment → Principal/Interest Tracking → Voucher → Ledger.
- Capital contribution → Approval → Capital Voucher → Equity Ledger.

## 7.3 Transaction Integrity

Every posted financial transaction must have:

- Unique Transaction ID.
- Company ID.
- Branch ID where applicable.
- Counter ID where applicable.
- Financial Year.
- Source Module.
- Source Transaction Reference.
- Voucher Reference.
- Debit Account.
- Credit Account.
- Amount.
- Transaction Date.
- Posting Date.
- Created By.
- Approved By where applicable.
- Audit History.

All related multi-table financial operations must use atomic database transactions.

Prevent duplicate accounting entries.

Maintain source-to-ledger traceability.

